## Evidence

- swift-format lint --recursive --strict Package.swift Sources Tests: exit 0.
- make validate-core: exit 0; boundary/dependency checks, 345 tests in 30 suites, and swift build passed.
- make validate-libssh2: exit 0; pinned static extension-safe artifact, public fork APIs, real client/server rekey, post-rekey channel, and reply-correlated global-request paths passed. Its printed count=-43 line is the intentional injected socket-failure case.
- make test-libssh2-source-gates: exit 0; tampered sources fail before extraction or patching.
- make native-apple-matrix: exit 0; Apple schemes plus dual-architecture extension-safe macOS provider/harness builds passed, and the stripped release harness contains HEV plus hidden libssh2 rekey linkage.
- Focused adapter suite: 7 tests passed, including hard callback bounds, partial drains, oversized-read rejection, signer EAGAIN, deferred capability disclosure, allocator recovery, and 20 repeated failed-handshake cleanup cycles returning channels, sessions, sockets, tasks, allocations, and buffers to zero.

## Explicit red/deferred evidence

The four M3-owned exact semantics remain unsupported rather than green. Live functional and scale SSH fixture execution is intentionally not claimed here because the task input assigns those fixtures to TASK-260715-39xz9g, the common suite to TASK-260715-2d3g5e, and the functional/rekey matrix to TASK-260715-1u2vpc. This outcome proves adapter implementation, bounded loopback seams, pinned-engine behavior, packaging, and Apple-target conformance without fabricating downstream selection evidence.

## Anomaly and resolution

The first Apple-matrix run exited 2 after Xcode copied the native-fixture and libssh2 library XCFramework module maps to the same Release/include/module.modulemap. Isolating only SYMROOT was insufficient within one graph. The final matrix retains Xcode device/simulator/native checks and uses separate dual-architecture SwiftPM application-extension builds for provider/harness targets; it exits 0. The decision is recorded in LOGBOOK.md.
